  "promotionalOffers": "Offers and promotions",
  "@promotionalOffers": {
    "description": "Heading for the GDPR Art 21 objection row. 🔴 THE VALUE IS THE PAGE'S WORDS, NOT THE OTHER WAY ROUND. sites/nikatru/privacy.html §3 (owner-signed 2026-08-10) tells the reader \"Open the app's Settings and turn Offers and promotions off\", and r4 had shipped this string as \"Promotional offers\" — a person following the published instruction would look for a heading that was not there. The page is the promise and is [ADR 031] class B owner copy; an app label is class A, so the app moved. Changed in apps/subscriptiontracker and in the brick, en and ta together. Named for what it governs (offers), not for the mechanism (consent), because the user is looking for the thing they read about."
  },
  "promoObjectionExplain": "You can stop seeing offers for our apps at any time. Nothing else changes.",
  "@promoObjectionExplain": {
    "description": "Art 21(4) requires the right to object to be presented clearly and separately. The second sentence is load-bearing: it says objecting costs nothing, so the control is not read as a trade."
  },
  "promoStopOffers": "Stop showing offers",
  "@promoStopOffers": {
    "description": "The objection action. NEVER confirm-shaming ('No thanks, I don't want to save money') — India's CCPA Dark Patterns Guidelines 2023 forbid it and research/44 V5 lists it as forbidden in all configurations."
  },
  "promoResumeOffers": "Show offers again",
  "@promoResumeOffers": {
    "description": "The way back. Art 21 gives a right to object, not a duty to stay objected, so a one-way control would be a trap."
  },
  "promoOffersOff": "Offers are off",
  "@promoOffersOff": {
    "description": "The objected state, stated as an effect. A control whose state the user cannot see is a button that appears to do nothing."
  },
  "promoLabel": "Promotion",
  "@promoLabel": {
    "description": "THE ONE STRING THE STORES ACTUALLY MANDATE. It is the visible label on PromoSurface, and one word satisfies Apple 2.5.18 ('must clearly indicate that they are an ad'), Microsoft 10.10.4 ('clearly distinguishable from other content'), Play's native-ads trigger and India's Disguised Advertisement dark pattern at once. It exists here with no consumer yet BECAUSE the first promotional card is the caller: PromoSurface's English default would otherwise ship untranslated, and a Tamil user would meet the one word a reviewer checks for in English."
  },
